  "era": "Ottoman-era village dress to UNESCO listing (2014)",
  "summary": "Deep-blue silk thread on homespun linen: Bosnian women stitched dress, faith and dowry in one hue.",
  "description": "On the Zmijanje plateau above the Vrbas river, women developed an embroidery of almost graphic-modern restraint — a single deep indigo-blue silk on natural linen, worked in counted threads into diamonds, crosses, spirals and miniature wheels over bridal dress, veils, pillowcases and bed hangings. The motifs carried ideas of fertility, protection and status through pure geometry; each garment became a record of the embroiderer's skill and family fortune. UNESCO inscribed the practice in 2014 as a living tradition passed from mother to daughter, now also stitched by diaspora women who mail patterns across continents.",
  "culturalContext": "Zmijanje embroidery stitched Bosnian village identity through Ottoman and Austro-Hungarian rule; UNESCO's 2014 inscription recognized its matrilineal transmission, and Bosnian diaspora women now sustain the practice as heritage identity — a blue thread connecting Vrbas-valley villages to Vienna and St. Louis.",
